{
  "duration": 392.2320000000104,
  "chapters": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 72.72000000000186
    },
    {
      "start": 72.72000000000186,
      "end": 146.78400000000377
    },
    {
      "start": 146.78400000000377,
      "end": 213.64800000000542
    },
    {
      "start": 213.64800000000542,
      "end": 301.41600000000784
    },
    {
      "start": 301.41600000000784,
      "end": 392.2320000000104
    }
  ],
  "cues": [
    {
      "start": 0,
      "end": 0.7599999904632568,
      "text": "Welcome back.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 1.2999999523162842,
      "end": 4.340000152587891,
      "text": "In straight line motion, we described how velocity changes.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 5.039999961853027,
      "end": 6.639999866485596,
      "text": "Today we ask why it changes.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 7.579999923706055,
      "end": 9.319999694824219,
      "text": "Imagine pushing a box across a floor.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 10.020000457763672,
      "end": 13.4399995803833,
      "text": "Before writing an equation, choose the box as the body you are studying.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 14.319999694824219,
      "end": 19.34000015258789,
      "text": "Draw its weight downward, the floor's normal reaction upward, your push to the",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 19.34000015258789,
      "end": 22.780000686645508,
      "text": "right, and friction to the left if the box tends to slide right.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 23.040000915527344,
      "end": 24.899999618530273,
      "text": "This is a free body diagram.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 25.6200008392334,
      "end": 27.860000610351562,
      "text": "Every arrow must name a real interaction.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 28.579999923706055,
      "end": 31.68000030517578,
      "text": "Do not draw an extra arrow called motion or acceleration.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 32.779998779296875,
      "end": 34.439998626708984,
      "text": "Acceleration is the result we calculate.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 35.34000015258789,
      "end": 36.86000061035156,
      "text": "Now imagine the forces balance.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 37.63999938964844,
      "end": 38.7599983215332,
      "text": "Must the box be stationary?",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 39.47999954223633,
      "end": 40.380001068115234,
      "text": "Take a moment to think.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 40.959999084472656,
      "end": 41.29999923706055,
      "text": "No.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 42.060001373291016,
      "end": 46.439998626708984,
      "text": "Newton's first law says zero net force means constant velocity in an inertial",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 46.439998626708984,
      "end": 46.68000030517578,
      "text": "frame.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 47.29999923706055,
      "end": 50.68000030517578,
      "text": "That constant velocity may be zero, but it does not have to be.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 51.31999969482422,
      "end": 55.34000015258789,
      "text": "For ordinary classroom problems we usually treat the ground as approximately",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 55.34000015258789,
      "end": 55.86000061035156,
      "text": "inertial.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 56.58000183105469,
      "end": 60.68000030517578,
      "text": "A useful way to test your diagram is to ask who is exerting each force.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 61.47999954223633,
      "end": 62.2400016784668,
      "text": "Earth supplies weight.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 62.7400016784668,
      "end": 64.33999633789062,
      "text": "The floor supplies contact forces.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 64.91999816894531,
      "end": 66.30000305175781,
      "text": "Your hand supplies the push.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 66.94000244140625,
      "end": 71.05999755859375,
      "text": "In JEE problems, getting this picture right often matters more than the algebra",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 71.05999755859375,
      "end": 71.73999786376953,
      "text": "that follows.",
      "chapter": 0
    },
    {
      "start": 72.72000000000186,
      "end": 75.68000003814883,
      "text": "Newton's second law connects the diagram to motion.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 76.33999988556094,
      "end": 80.74000045776553,
      "text": "For a constant mass, the vector sum of external forces equals mass times",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 80.74000045776553,
      "end": 81.33999988556094,
      "text": "acceleration.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 82.36000034332461,
      "end": 83.53999969482608,
      "text": "Notice the word sum.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 84.28000041961856,
      "end": 87.87999984741397,
      "text": "One force alone does not tell you the acceleration when several forces act.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 88.69999954223819,
      "end": 89.7600009155292,
      "text": "Choose right as positive.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 90.42000076294131,
      "end": 95.66000053405948,
      "text": "A five kilogram box has a fourteen newton pull to the right and four newtons of",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 95.66000053405948,
      "end": 96.3599993896503,
      "text": "friction to the left.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 97.32000038147159,
      "end": 99.43999931335635,
      "text": "Before calculating, predict the direction.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 99.95999977112002,
      "end": 101.37999984741397,
      "text": "The rightward force wins.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 101.77999946594424,
      "end": 105.67999908447452,
      "text": "The net force is fourteen minus four, or ten newtons to the right.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 106.80000183105655,
      "end": 109.53999969482608,
      "text": "Divide by five kilograms to get two metres per second squared.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 110.3599993896503,
      "end": 112.62000152588077,
      "text": "We subtract because the forces oppose each other.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 113.51999923706241,
      "end": 115.1000010681171,
      "text": "Now separate the vertical direction.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 115.74000045776553,
      "end": 119.31999847412295,
      "text": "If the box has no vertical acceleration, and only weight and the normal",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 119.31999847412295,
      "end": 122.80000183105655,
      "text": "reaction act vertically, the normal reaction equals its weight.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 123.2600009155292,
      "end": 125.80000183105655,
      "text": "That conclusion follows from the vertical equation.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 126.37999984741397,
      "end": 127.74000045776553,
      "text": "It is not a universal rule.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 128.31999847412294,
      "end": 131.53999969482607,
      "text": "An upward angled pull would reduce the floor's required reaction.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 132.1799990844745,
      "end": 134.5199992370624,
      "text": "A lift accelerating upward would change it too.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 134.5199992370624,
      "end": 140.34000274658388,
      "text": "Here is your exam routine: draw the body, choose axes, resolve forces, add",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 140.34000274658388,
      "end": 142.59999725341982,
      "text": "signed components, then divide by mass.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 143.59999725341982,
      "end": 146.38079804687726,
      "text": "Check that the acceleration points in the direction of the net force, even when",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 146.38079804687726,
      "end": 146.78400000000377,
      "text": "the body is currently moving the other way.",
      "chapter": 1
    },
    {
      "start": 146.78400000000377,
      "end": 151.90399988556285,
      "text": "If every force has an equal and opposite partner, why does anything accelerate?",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 152.74400003815074,
      "end": 154.4839998092689,
      "text": "The answer is in the body you selected.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 155.22399958038707,
      "end": 159.603999694828,
      "text": "When your hand pushes a box, the box pushes your hand with equal magnitude and",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 159.603999694828,
      "end": 160.46400030517955,
      "text": "opposite direction.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 161.18399961853405,
      "end": 163.74399908447643,
      "text": "The force on the box belongs on the box's diagram.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 164.34399946594615,
      "end": 167.02399977112194,
      "text": "The force on your hand belongs on your hand's diagram.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 167.68399961853405,
      "end": 170.06400068664928,
      "text": "They do not cancel in the net force on the box alone.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 170.86399992370983,
      "end": 172.36399992370983,
      "text": "Try a common JEE trap.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 172.82400091553112,
      "end": 174.20400007629772,
      "text": "A book rests on a table.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 174.20400007629772,
      "end": 178.06400068664928,
      "text": "Its weight points downward and the normal reaction points upward.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 178.86400183105846,
      "end": 180.0040012207069,
      "text": "Are these a third law pair?",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 181.0040012207069,
      "end": 183.103999694828,
      "text": "Think about which bodies receive the forces.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 184.08399923706432,
      "end": 187.48400076294323,
      "text": "Both forces act on the book, so they are not partners under the third law.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 188.32400091553112,
      "end": 191.4239993896522,
      "text": "The partner of Earth's gravitational pull on the book is the book's",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 191.4239993896522,
      "end": 192.62400015259166,
      "text": "gravitational pull on Earth.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 193.02400167847057,
      "end": 196.90399893188854,
      "text": "The partner of the table's contact force on the book is the book's contact",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 196.90399893188854,
      "end": 198.52400167847057,
      "text": "force on the table.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 198.52400167847057,
      "end": 201.24399908447643,
      "text": "Equal magnitudes do not by themselves identify a pair.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 201.7639995422401,
      "end": 204.30400045776744,
      "text": "Name the interaction and reverse the giver and receiver.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 205.0040012207069,
      "end": 206.22399862671276,
      "text": "That is the reliable test.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 206.9239993896522,
      "end": 211.24399908447643,
      "text": "The two forces act simultaneously; the words action and reaction do not mean",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 211.24399908447643,
      "end": 212.48399694824596,
      "text": "that one force happens later.",
      "chapter": 2
    },
    {
      "start": 213.64800000000542,
      "end": 218.30799984741753,
      "text": "Friction is where a correct formula can still give a wrong answer if you choose",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 218.30799984741753,
      "end": 219.32799982834405,
      "text": "the wrong situation.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 220.48800015259332,
      "end": 223.98800015259332,
      "text": "Static friction adjusts to prevent relative slipping, up to a limit.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 224.6680004577691,
      "end": 226.56800007629937,
      "text": "It does not always equal that limit.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 227.90800022888726,
      "end": 232.06800007629937,
      "text": "Consider a four kilogram block initially at rest on a horizontal floor.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 232.7079994659478,
      "end": 236.8879997711236,
      "text": "Use g equal to ten metres per second squared, as specified in this example.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 237.58800053406304,
      "end": 239.74800038147515,
      "text": "The normal reaction is forty newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 239.74800038147515,
      "end": 243.92800068665093,
      "text": "Let the static friction coefficient be zero point five and the kinetic",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 243.92800068665093,
      "end": 245.56800007629937,
      "text": "coefficient zero point three.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 246.30799984741753,
      "end": 248.56799816895074,
      "text": "The maximum static friction is twenty newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 249.34800076294488,
      "end": 250.70800137329644,
      "text": "Now push with twelve newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 251.38800167847222,
      "end": 251.84800076294488,
      "text": "Will it move?",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 252.90799832153863,
      "end": 254.56799816895074,
      "text": "Pause and compare twelve with the limit.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 255.60799908447808,
      "end": 259.3880016784722,
      "text": "Static friction can supply twelve newtons in the opposite direction, so the",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 259.3880016784722,
      "end": 260.0879986267144,
      "text": "block stays at rest.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 260.6480000000054,
      "end": 264.06799816895074,
      "text": "Its actual friction is twelve, not twenty.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 264.06799816895074,
      "end": 266.2280018310601,
      "text": "Next increase the push to twenty eight newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 266.2280018310601,
      "end": 271.5879986267144,
      "text": "Rest is no longer possible because the required friction exceeds twenty.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 271.5879986267144,
      "end": 276.02800106812066,
      "text": "Once the block slides, use kinetic friction: zero point three times forty",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 276.02800106812066,
      "end": 277.06799816895074,
      "text": "equals twelve newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 278.18800091553277,
      "end": 281.4279987793023,
      "text": "The net force is twenty eight minus twelve, or sixteen newtons.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 282.1480000000054,
      "end": 285.7280018310601,
      "text": "Divide by four kilograms and the acceleration is four metres per second",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 285.7280018310601,
      "end": 286.06799816895074,
      "text": "squared.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 287.4080021362359,
      "end": 290.508000610357,
      "text": "Friction opposes relative slipping at the contact, or its tendency.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 291.2280018310601,
      "end": 296.008000610357,
      "text": "It does not always oppose the body's overall motion: static friction can push a",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 296.008000610357,
      "end": 296.88799786377496,
      "text": "walking person forward.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 297.88799786377496,
      "end": 301.128003356939,
      "text": "Always identify the contact and its slipping tendency first.",
      "chapter": 3
    },
    {
      "start": 301.41600000000784,
      "end": 304.8960000190813,
      "text": "On a slope, a better choice of axes makes the problem simpler.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 305.75600015259573,
      "end": 308.95599996186087,
      "text": "Choose one axis down the slope and another perpendicular to it.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 309.63600026703665,
      "end": 311.47600041962454,
      "text": "Weight still points vertically downward.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 312.115999809273,
      "end": 317.155999771126,
      "text": "Its component down the slope is m g sine theta, and its component into the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 317.155999771126,
      "end": 321.8360000763018,
      "text": "slope is m g cosine theta, where theta is the incline's angle to the horizontal.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 322.7159992370684,
      "end": 326.63599931336233,
      "text": "The normal reaction balances the perpendicular component if no other",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 326.63599931336233,
      "end": 329.69600068665335,
      "text": "perpendicular forces act and there is no perpendicular acceleration.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 330.1760002288897,
      "end": 332.89599954224417,
      "text": "It therefore equals m g cosine theta.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 333.75600015259573,
      "end": 337.3759990844805,
      "text": "Let us solve a two kilogram block sliding on a frictionless thirty degree",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 337.3759990844805,
      "end": 339.41600000000784,
      "text": "incline, using g equal to ten.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 340.23599969483206,
      "end": 342.6959987793047,
      "text": "First predict whether the acceleration depends on mass.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 343.3759990844805,
      "end": 348.14599954224417,
      "text": "The downslope force is two times ten times one",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 348.14599954224417,
      "end": 351.2760006103594,
      "text": "half, or ten newtons.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 351.91600000000784,
      "end": 355.49600183106253,
      "text": "Divide by two kilograms to get five metres per second squared down the slope.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 356.25600015259573,
      "end": 356.8759990844805,
      "text": "Mass cancels.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 356.8759990844805,
      "end": 361.5359989318926,
      "text": "The normal reaction is twenty times cosine thirty degrees, which is ten times",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 361.5359989318926,
      "end": 364.39599954224417,
      "text": "the square root of three newtons, about seventeen point three.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 365.0559993896563,
      "end": 367.43599664307425,
      "text": "It is perpendicular to the slope, not vertical.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 368.0760036621172,
      "end": 372.1559978637774,
      "text": "If the block slides downward with kinetic friction, subtract mu k times the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 372.1559978637774,
      "end": 375.4560009155352,
      "text": "normal reaction from the downslope force before dividing by mass.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 376.13600122071097,
      "end": 379.39600335694143,
      "text": "An upslope acceleration can slow a block that is still moving downward.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 380.5360027465899,
      "end": 385.0960003051836,
      "text": "Finish every force problem with four checks: one body, clear axes, correct",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 385.0960003051836,
      "end": 387.13600122071097,
      "text": "contact model, and consistent units.",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 388.0360027465899,
      "end": 391.33599816895315,
      "text": "Complete the practice questions next, and keep the written lesson open for the",
      "chapter": 4
    },
    {
      "start": 391.33599816895315,
      "end": 391.8759990844805,
      "text": "worked solutions.",
      "chapter": 4
    }
  ]
}
